Wait—there's a subtle issue with treefiles + `rawexpread_fully`. Look at the read loop:

```c
while(ctx->current_len > 0) {
    buf = find_read_buf(ctx);
    if((ret = rawexpread(...)) <= 0) {
        break;
    }
    confirm_read(client, ctx, ret);
    ctx->current_offset += ret;
    ctx->current_len -= ret;
}
return (ret < 0 || ctx->current_len != 0);
```

rawexpread is clamped to the block boundary by maxbytes, so each iteration advances at most up to the end of the block. open_treefile extends newly-created/dummy files to TREEPAGESIZE (4096), so pread won't return a short read within a block... except in one case: for a block file previously created via O_RDWR and extended to 4096, pread returns the full amount. Dummy file: extended to 4096. Therefore pread always returns the requested `len`. Good—the loop terminates.

But wait: what about reads of existing block files that were created by older means and are smaller than TREEPAGESIZE (e.g., hand-made files)? pread returns a short count → the loop continues from the next offset, which falls into the same block file → the read returns 0 → break with error. Edge case, upstream has the same behavior. Not a problem.

Also note: `rawexpread` in treefiles READONLY mode: open_treefile(O_RDONLY) creates an unlinked dummy file → zero-filled. Reads on a fresh treefiles export return zeros. ✓ This is consistent with the behavior of regular sparse files.

